	On the subject of poly-wands, I held my first poly-party
recently, wanting to finally reach demi-god-hood, and was surprised at
how easy it was.  Starting with a poly-wand with 9 charges, and with
the help of a ghost level, I built a poly-pile with about 15 wands,
about 20 tools, and a bunch of weapons and armor, all next to a lawful
altar.  By the time I was finished, about six hours later, I had armor
rating -35 with boots of speed, shield of reflection, crystal plate
mail, Hawaiian shirt, gauntlets of power, cloak of magic protection
and helm of telepathy; +6 Mjollnir and +8 Excalibur (inherited from
the ghost); 5 amulets of life-saving; 3 fully charged wands of wishing
(created with 0 charges and charged with blessed scrolls of charging);
250 hit points (obtained by quaffing blessed potions of extra
healing); all attributes maxed out (from blessed potions of gain
ability); luck maxed out forever (from a luckstone and some
sacrificing); Hand of Elbereth; every useful ring; a stethoscope;
every useful spell except genocide; and experience level 14 (from
potions of gain level).  Not to mention countless lembas wafers and
food rations.  From that point winning the game was so easy it was
actually disappointing.  Each time the wiz showed I just wished up a
cockatrice corpse; when I got stuck in the top level of the tower and
couldn't figure out how to get out I just wished up a cursed potion of
gain level.  Ho hum.

	The thing that is most abusable is that under the right
circumstances polymorphing is actually _generative_:  it creates
limitless numbers of NEW objects.  This occurs because tools sometimes
get turned into bags or boxes or chests, which are often created with
scrolls or potions or even wands inside; also they are sometimes
turned into bags of tricks, which can be taken to an altar and applied
to create monsters for sacrificing, sometimes leaving behind objects
as an extra bonus.  Nymphs and dwarves usually leave behind mirrors
and pickaxes, which can be added to the pile and turned into boxes
etc.  Once you have about 50 tools in the pile you get new wands of
polymorph faster than you use them up, so the process can go on as
long as you have the patience to keep it up.

	I think that getting good weapons and armor is a reasonable
payoff for the work of building a poly-pile, but the inexaustible
supply of scrolls and potions and wands is not.  There are two
reasonable solutions.  One is to make it so that when boxes, chests,
and bags are created by polymorph, they are always empty; the other,
which I think I like better, is David Hairston's proposal for
polymorphed objects to have a 5-10% chance of disintegrating, so that
the number of tools in the pile will gradually decrease.

	Something definitely ought to be done.  I don't want to take
an oath against polymorphing, because it's fun, but under the present
rules it's just too easy.

	-- Bill Skaggs


